    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 131807\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1007 AM PST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Unsettled weather returns to the region today and lasts through \n  Saturday\n\n* Additional precipitation and cooler weather arrives Monday and \n  Tuesday and again in the second half of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through next Thursday\n\nThe pattern changes today as a trough and low pressure dip into \nthe region with strong southerly winds building into the mid to \nlate morning with precipitation close to follow the initial wave \nof stronger winds. Wind Advisories are in effect Friday into early \nSaturday with 20 to 30 mph sustained southerly winds and gusts to \n55 mph. The first areas affected will be the eastern Sierra by the\nlate morning. A Winter Storm Warning is in effect for elevations \nabove 7,000 feet in the Eastern Sierra for Friday into Saturday \nafternoon. Around a foot of new snow will be possible in the \nSierra Crest, and 5 to 10 inches of snow expected between 7,000 ft\nand 9,500 ft. Rain chances and winds spread across the region \ninto Friday afternoon. Rain shadowing and a poor moisture feed \nwill prevent much in the way of rainfall in the desert valleys, \nbut still offer some measurable amounts for higher elevations. The\nstrongest winds will favor high elevations, and will spread to \nthe lower valleys in the eastern portions of the CWA, but the \nwestern half wont see the hazardously windy conditions. Winds ease\nthrough Saturday and rain looks to exit that night, leading to \ncalmer conditions for Sunday.\n\nBack to unsettled weather for Monday as a low pressure builds into \nthe area early that morning and spreads through the day. The \nbetter feed of moisture for this system doesn't line up until \nafter the low pressure center crosses the Sierra, limiting Sierra \nsnow potential, but leading to better rainfall for the rest of the\nregion. Rain looks to become widespread into Monday afternoon for\nthe areas east of the Sierra Nevada, but rain rates look to stay \nlight to moderate. Rainfall totals from Monday into Tuesday look \nmodest (0.10 to 0.25\") for all but Northeast Clark Co, Mohave Co, \nand Lincoln Co which will range from 0.30 to 1.0\" after showers \nexit to the east. Wednesday will offer another pause in rain, but \nanother batch of showers arrives early Thursday and could linger \ninto next Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Gusty \nsoutheasterly winds develop late this morning, intensifying and \nturning to the southwest this afternoon with peak gusts between 20 \nand 25 knots. These winds should become less gusty but remain over \n10 knots in the evening, then strengthen again in the early morning \nhours. Although precipitation is not expected within the vicinity of \nthe terminal, showers may develop over high terrain around the Las \nVegas Valley, which could also lead to terrain obscuration. SCT to \nBKN clouds at or above 10kft persist throughout the forecast\nperiod.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Expect widespread gusty \nwinds this afternoon and tonight. Some of the strongest winds will \nbe in the Owens Valley including KBIH, where southeasterly gusts \nwill peak between 30 and 35 knots this afternoon. These winds will \nshift to the north overnight and a few showers within the vicinity \nof the terminal cannot be ruled out. Elsewhere, gusts peak out of \nthe southwest between 20 and 25 knots in the Las Vegas Valley and \nColorado River Valley, and out of the west between 30 and 35 knots \nat KDAG. Clouds will be SCT to BKN around 10kft but lower bases may \noccur with any showers that develop. Terrain obscuration is expected \nwith rain and snow showers over area mountains.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Murdock\nAVIATION...Meltzer\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
